QuickJS-ng disables its native stack-overflow check on WASI, so deep
recursion (JS calls, JSON.parse, RegExp compilation, nested toString, ...)
keeps pushing frames until the wasm shadow stack runs below address 0 and the
module traps with "out of bounds memory access", killing the instance.

The check works on WASI as on native targets: __builtin_frame_address(0)
returns the shadow-stack pointer and the stack grows down. Enforce the limit
set with JS_SetMaxStackSize (kafig-runtime sets it inside the 1 MiB shadow
stack) so overflow raises a catchable RangeError instead.

--- a/quickjs/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs/quickjs.c
@@ -2588,15 +2588,13 @@
 
 static void update_stack_limit(JSRuntime *rt)
 {
-#if defined(__wasi__)
-    rt->stack_limit = 0; /* no limit */
-#else
-    if (rt->stack_size == 0) {
+    if (rt->stack_size == 0 || rt->stack_size >= rt->stack_top) {
+        /* kafig: also guards the subtraction below; the shadow stack on
+           wasm32 starts at address 0, so the limit must stay positive. */
         rt->stack_limit = 0; /* no limit */
     } else {
         rt->stack_limit = rt->stack_top - rt->stack_size;
     }
-#endif
 }
 
 void JS_SetMaxStackSize(JSRuntime *rt, size_t stack_size)
